<h1>Sessions</h1>
<p>Everything the tutorial has built up in the controller so far is one <strong>session</strong>: the items loaded
	into the tree, the values planned for each test, the groups taken out of the plan, the endpoints picked, and
	the runs with their results. The session is the controller's workspace, and closing the window would lose it,
	so the controller saves it to a file and loads it back. A saved session is how a lab's setup outlives the
	evening it was made: the same tests, the same plans, the same machines, ready the next morning or on
	another controller.</p>
<p>A session file is a JSON file in the <code>Sessions</code> folder next to the controller's <code>Logs</code>.
	It records the loaded items by their paths, the plan and the draft edits of every test, which items are
	unplanned, the runs and their results, and the shape of the tree. It does not copy the suites: loading a
	session reads the tests again from their files, so a suite edited in the meantime comes back edited.</p>

<h2 id="the-unsaved-session">The unsaved session</h2>
<p>The tree's top row names the session. A controller starts with <strong>New Session</strong>, and the star after
	the name says it holds changes that are not in any file. Load the tutorial suite, add the Structure and
	Lifecycle group and the Hello World test as items of their own, run the first two status tests and unplan the
	Advanced Configuration Parameters group, and the row reads:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/sessions/unsaved-session.png", "Top of the Test Explorer with the Session row labelled New Session followed by a star, a plan of 156 with 32 done, and beneath it the Orcraft Tutorial suite, the Structure and Lifecycle group item and the Hello World test item", "large") }}
<p>The star appears on any change that a save would record: an item added or removed, a plan updated, a row
	planned or unplanned, a run started. It is the cue that closing the controller now would lose something.</p>

<h2 id="saving">Saving</h2>
<p>Click <strong>Save</strong> in the <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/execution-tab/#new-save-and-load">Session
	action frame</a>. A file dialog opens in the <code>Sessions</code> folder with the session's name proposed;
	give the file a name that says what the session is for, here <code>statuses-lab</code>. The controller
	confirms where it wrote:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/sessions/session-saved-notice.png", "Session Saved notice reading Session saved to C:\\orcraft\\Sessions\\statuses-lab.json", "small") }}
<p>The session row takes the file's name and loses its star:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/sessions/saved-session.png", "Top of the Test Explorer with the Session row now labelled statuses-lab without a star and the same three items beneath it", "large") }}
<p>From now on the name is the session's, and the star tracks changes against the file. Unplan one more group
	and it is back:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/sessions/modified-session.png", "Top of the Test Explorer with the Session row labelled statuses-lab followed by a star and the plan reduced to 147", "large") }}
<p>Save again to write the file over. A session that is running tests can be saved too: the file records the
	running tests as stopped and the queued ones as dequeued, since neither can be resumed from a file.</p>

<h2 id="starting-over">Starting over</h2>
<p><strong>New</strong> empties the tree. With unsaved changes on the row, the controller asks first, because
	the work would be lost:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/sessions/new-session-confirm.png", "New Session dialog warning that starting a new session removes all items and discards unsaved changes, and asking whether to continue, with Yes and No buttons", "small") }}
<p>Confirm, and the tree holds only the session row, back to <strong>New Session</strong>, and the
	<strong>Add Item</strong> placeholder:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/sessions/empty-session.png", "Empty Test Explorer with the Session row labelled New Session with a star, zero counts, and an Add Item link reading Browse for Batch/Suite/Group/Test file", "large") }}

<h2 id="loading">Loading</h2>
<p><strong>Load</strong> opens the same file dialog on the <code>Sessions</code> folder. Pick
	<code>statuses-lab.json</code>. The controller reads every item from its files again, restores the plans,
	the unplanned rows and the runs, and the tree is as it was at the save:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/sessions/loaded-session.png", "Execution tree after loading statuses-lab: the Session row named statuses-lab without a star, the Advanced Configuration Parameters group greyed as unplanned, and the Status: Pass with Warning and Status: Fail tests with their run rows, statuses and times restored", "large") }}
<p>The two runs are back with their statuses, times and logs, so the Details dialog and the log pane work on
	them as before. The Advanced Configuration Parameters group is still unplanned, and the plan count is the
	saved one. The Basic Configuration Parameters group is planned again, because it was unplanned after the
	save and that change was never written.</p>
<p>Loading into a session that has running tests stops them first, after a confirmation, since the tree is
	replaced whole. Loading never merges: to combine two sessions, load one and add the other's items with
	<strong>Add Item</strong>.</p>
<p>The session file is also what the controller keeps for itself between launches: closing the window and
	opening it again brings the last state back, named or not. Saving gives that state a name and a file that
	can be kept, copied to another controller, or loaded next to other sessions of the same lab.</p>

<p>The next page, <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/exporting-test-batch-for-ci/">Exporting Test Batch for
	CI</a>, takes a composed session out of the controller altogether, into a form a pipeline runs.</p>
